# 001200-001299/20250401T171705Z_1283460145.wiki
zΔ⚡b|=3925|-1|+%3D%3D%3D Context format %3D%3D%3D%0AThe [[BSD%7CBerkeley distribution of Unix]] made a point of adding the ''context format'' ({{code%7C%2Dc}}) and the ability to recurse on filesystem directory structures ({{code%7C%2Dr}}), adding those features in 2.8 BSD, released in July 1981. The context format of diff introduced at Berkeley helped with distributing patches for source code that may have been changed minimally.%0A%0AIn the context format, any changed lines are shown alongside unchanged lines before and after. The inclusion of any number of unchanged lines provides a ''context'' to the patch. The ''context'' consists of lines that have not changed between the two files and serve as a reference to locate the lines' place in a modified file and find the intended location for a change to be applied regardless of whether the line numbers still correspond. The context format introd|=1|+ces grea|=1|+er re|=1|+dability for human|=1|+ and reliability when applying the patch, and an output which is accepted as input to the [[patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]] program. This intelligent behavior is not possible with the traditional diff output.%0A%0AThe number of unchanged lines shown above and below a change ''hunk'' can be defined by the user, even zero, but three lines is typically the default. If the context of unchanged lines in a hunk overlap with an adjacent hunk, then diff will avoid duplicating the unchanged lines and merge the hunks into a single hunk.%0A%0AA "{{code%7C!}}" represents a change between lines that correspond in the two files, whereas a "{{code%7C%2B}}" represents the addition of a line, and a "{{code%7C%2D}}" the removal of a line. A blank [[space (punctuation)%7Cspace]] represents an unchanged line. At the beginning of the patch is the file information, including the full path and a [[time stamp]] delimited by a tab character. At the beginning of each hunk are the line numbers that apply for the corresponding change in the files. A number range appearing between sets of three asterisks applies to the original file, while sets of three dashes apply to the new file. The hunk ranges specify the starting and ending line numbers in the respective file.%0A%0AThe command {{code%7Cdiff %2Dc original new}} produces the following output:%0A<syntaxhighlight lang%3D"diff">%0A*** /path/to/original%09timestamp%0A%2D%2D%2D /path/to/new%09timestamp%0A***************%0A*** 1,3 ****%0A%2D%2D%2D 1,9 %2D%2D%2D%2D%0A%2B This is an important%0A%2B notice! It should%0A%2B therefore be located at%0A%2B the beginning of this%0A%2B document!%0A%2B%0A  This part of the%0A  document has stayed the%0A  same from version to%0A***************%0A*** 8,20 ****%0A  compress the size of the%0A  changes.%0A%0A%2D This paragraph contains%0A%2D text that is outdated.%0A%2D It will be deleted in the%0A%2D near future.%0A%0A  It is important to spell%0A! check this dokument. On%0A  the other hand, a%0A  misspelled word isn't%0A  the end of the world.%0A%2D%2D%2D 14,21 %2D%2D%2D%2D%0A  compress the size of the%0A  changes.%0A%0A  It is important to spell%0A! check this document. On%0A  the other hand, a%0A  misspelled word isn't%0A  the end of the world.%0A***************%0A*** 22,24 ****%0A%2D%2D%2D 23,29 %2D%2D%2D%2D%0A  this paragraph needs to%0A  be changed. Things can%0A  be added after it.%0A%2B%0A%2B This paragraph contains%0A%2B important new additions%0A%2B to this document.%0A</syntaxhighlight>%0A'''Note''': ''Here, the diff output is shown with colors to make it easier to read. The diff utility does not produce colored output; its output is [[plain text]]. However, many tools can show the output with colors by using [[syntax highlighting]].''|=5aa6|
