zΔ⚡b|=4649|+The ''unified format'' (or ''unidiff'')<ref>{{Cite web%7Curl%3Dhttps://www.gnu.org/software/diffutils/manual/html_node/Detailed%2DUnified.html%7Ctitle%3DDetailed Description of Unified Format%7Cwebsite%3DGNU Diffutils (version 3.7, 7 January 2018)%7Caccess%2Ddate%3D29 January 2020%7Carchive%2Ddate%3D18 January 2020%7Carchive%2Durl%3Dhttps://web.archive.org/web/20200118142136/http://www.gnu.org/software/diffutils/manual/html_node/Detailed%2DUnified.html%7Curl%2Dstatus%3Dlive}}</ref>|=afe|+.%0A%0AThe|=1|+hunk range for the original should be the sum of all contextual and deletion (including changed) hunk lines. The hunk range for the new file should be a sum of all contextual and addition (including changed) hunk lines. If hunk size information does not correspond with the number of lines in the hunk, then the diff could be considered invalid and be rejected.%0A%0AOptionally, the hunk range can be followed by the heading of the section or function that the hunk is part of. This is mainly useful to make the diff easier to read. When creating a diff with GNU diff, the heading is identified by [[regular expression]] matching.<ref>[https://www.gnu.org/software/diffutils/manual/html_node/Sections.html 2.2.3 Showing Which Sections Differences Are in], GNU diffutils manual</ref>|=492b|
